From: Grische <github@grische.xyz>
Date: Mon, 17 Aug 2026 10:47:04 +0200
Subject: MIPS: dts: intel-mips: drop the dead built-in DTB obj-y rule

The intel-mips dts Makefile carried

  obj-$(CONFIG_BUILTIN_DTB) += $(addsuffix .o, $(dtb-y))

which has never had any effect. arch/mips/boot/dts/Makefile pulls its
subdirectories in with subdir-y, not obj-y, so scripts/Makefile.build
descends with need-builtin unset and never makes built-in.a a target
for the directory - the listed objects are neither built nor linked.

Built-in DTBs reach vmlinux through a different path entirely:
arch/mips/Kconfig selects BUILTIN_DTB_ALL if BUILTIN_DTB, which feeds
arch/mips/boot/dts/dtbs-list into .builtin-dtbs.S in
scripts/Makefile.vmlinux.

A full build of this platform confirms it: the directory contains the
generated .dtb and dtbs-list, and no .o or built-in.a is produced
anywhere under arch/mips/boot/dts. No other arch/mips/boot/dts/*/Makefile
carries this pattern.

This matters beyond tidiness. BUILTIN_DTB_ALL embeds every DTB that
gets built, each behind a generated __dtb_<name>_begin symbol, so "one
built-in DTB per kernel" holds only because each Kconfig choice entry
gates one dtb-y line. Anyone reading this Makefile to work out how a
second board would be delivered needs the real mechanism visible, not a
dead rule suggesting another one.

Assisted-by: Claude:claude-opus-5
Signed-off-by: Grische <github@grische.xyz>

diff --git a/arch/mips/boot/dts/intel-mips/Makefile b/arch/mips/boot/dts/intel-mips/Makefile
index c7483ffc6c19..3d2176ca6a28 100644
--- a/arch/mips/boot/dts/intel-mips/Makefile
+++ b/arch/mips/boot/dts/intel-mips/Makefile
@@ -1,5 +1,3 @@
 # SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-2.0-only
 dtb-$(CONFIG_DTB_INTEL_MIPS_GRX500)	+= easy350_anywan.dtb
 dtb-$(CONFIG_DTB_INTEL_MIPS_FRITZ7560)	+= seale_avm_fritz7560.dtb
-
-obj-$(CONFIG_BUILTIN_DTB)		+= $(addsuffix .o, $(dtb-y))
